Taken in two parts, SIDE=frontend then SIDE=backend, against committed code.

The backend half ran twice. Its first attempt scored one mutant INCONCLUSIVE
rather than killed:

  the register loses a reportable producer                     INCONCLUSIVE -- 2 errors in 1.07s

Flipping SENSOR-FAULT from REPORTABLE to INTERMEDIATE leaves a `Producer` with
no measured background, which `Producer.__post_init__` refuses -- so the module
raised on import, the suite reported collection errors rather than a failing
test, and the harness correctly declined to read that as a kill. README.md's
EIGHTH hazard, met a second time.

The two instances break the same invariant class from opposite directions. The
first, `anom-016`'s sentinel, set a threshold to 99 dB and broke an ORDERING
constraint. This one broke a MUTUAL-EXCLUSION constraint: a reportable producer
may carry no band and an intermediate one must. Both looked like ordinary
one-line edits. The rule the hazard already states -- construct each mutant
before running the table -- is the one that catches either, and it was not
followed when this table was written.

Done explicitly on the retry: the replacement carries
`background=Background(low=0, high=0, runs=60)`, the mutated module was imported
on its own and its register printed before anything was run against it, which
showed the reportable set reduced to two members as intended.

