Three results worth reading rather than counting.

`the timeline words a conclusion its own way` is the mutant these three items
exist as one piece of work for: it gives the strip its own phrasing for a type
the summary already words, and only a test reading one label from both panels
can kill it. It did, on 2 tests.

`the primitive throws rather than refusing` took 36 of 88 tests down. That blast
radius IS the argument recorded on the component: a primitive that raises during
render takes a whole screen with it, which is a larger failure than the one it
was written to prevent.

`anything at all counts as a level` killed 19 where `any string counts as a
level` killed 9, and the gap is the shape of the guard. Widening the domain to
any string still refuses numbers, so only the non-numeric cases notice; removing
the check entirely lets 87 reach the DOM and every digit assertion fires.

18 of the 58 frontend tests had passed against a raising stub -- the non-numeric
refusals have no digit to find, and a stub rendering its label and a valid level
satisfies those halves by accident. With no coverage tooling on that side, this
pass is the only instrument that could say whether they hold anything up.
